WEBVTT

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.000
Two people want the same thing:

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.240
a simple dashboard of their team's weekly

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.020
sales.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.920
Alex types five words.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.730
Make me a sales dashboard.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.030
The agent builds something with charts.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.550
But the numbers are fake.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.990
It shows months,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.010
not weeks.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.000
It uses a chart library Alex has

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:22.930
never heard of.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.530
Two hours of fixes later,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:25.610
Alex gives up.

00:00:26.251 --> 00:00:28.451
Maya opens the same agent and writes

00:00:28.451 --> 00:00:29.721
a few clear lines.

00:00:30.071 --> 00:00:32.201
Connect to our real sales data.

00:00:32.471 --> 00:00:34.331
Show the last eight weeks as a

00:00:34.331 --> 00:00:35.471
stacked bar chart,

00:00:35.471 --> 00:00:36.981
one colour per person.

00:00:37.631 --> 00:00:39.931
Use the chart library we already have.

00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:42.201
Match our blue header and white cards.

00:00:42.211 --> 00:00:44.731
The agent builds exactly that.

00:00:44.731 --> 00:00:45.901
First try.

00:00:45.971 --> 00:00:46.971
Same agent,

00:00:46.971 --> 00:00:47.901
same goal.

00:00:47.911 --> 00:00:50.061
The only difference was the instructions.

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:53.263
A good instruction has three parts.

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:54.613
What you want,

00:00:54.613 --> 00:00:55.863
said specifically,

00:00:55.913 --> 00:00:57.853
because when an agent guesses,

00:00:57.853 --> 00:00:59.093
it guesses confidently.

00:00:59.093 --> 00:01:00.593
Why it matters,

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:02.393
so it understands the problem,

00:01:02.393 --> 00:01:03.653
not just the feature.

00:01:03.653 --> 00:01:05.263
And how you'll know it worked.

00:01:05.313 --> 00:01:07.133
That's the part most people skip,

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.353
and it's the one that lets the

00:01:08.353 --> 00:01:09.823
agent check its own work.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:12.360
Then say what not to do.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.720
Agents are eager.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.650
Ask one to fix a button colour,

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.980
and it might redesign the whole screen.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.450
Constraints are lane markers on a road.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.600
Don't change the existing pages.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:24.140
Don't add new tools.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.360
Use the colours we already have.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:27.650
Inside the lanes,

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.010
the agent drives freely.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.820
It just can't cross the lines.

00:01:31.576 --> 00:01:33.356
Instructions come in levels.

00:01:33.356 --> 00:01:35.206
Level zero is a wish:

00:01:35.256 --> 00:01:36.526
make the app better.

00:01:36.556 --> 00:01:38.276
Level one is a goal:

00:01:38.276 --> 00:01:40.166
add a way to export data.

00:01:40.436 --> 00:01:42.126
Level two is a specification:

00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:44.416
an export button on the settings page,

00:01:44.416 --> 00:01:46.836
with these columns and this file name.

00:01:46.836 --> 00:01:48.616
Level three is a full blueprint,

00:01:48.616 --> 00:01:49.856
usually more than you need.

00:01:49.856 --> 00:01:50.996
For most tasks,

00:01:50.996 --> 00:01:52.386
level two is the sweet spot.

00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:54.386
And before anything gets built,

00:01:54.386 --> 00:01:55.526
ask for a plan.

00:01:55.526 --> 00:01:57.006
Don't change anything yet.

00:01:57.006 --> 00:01:58.066
Tell me what you'll build,

00:01:58.066 --> 00:01:59.246
and how you'll check it.

00:01:59.246 --> 00:02:00.786
Then read that plan the way you'd

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.616
read a builder's quote for a kitchen.

00:02:02.626 --> 00:02:05.076
It's the cheapest review you'll ever do.

00:02:05.106 --> 00:02:06.886
A wrong assumption in a ten line

00:02:06.886 --> 00:02:09.006
plan costs one sentence to fix.

00:02:09.006 --> 00:02:10.206
Found after the build,

00:02:10.206 --> 00:02:11.516
it costs an afternoon.

00:02:12.456 --> 00:02:13.936
When something is still off,

00:02:13.936 --> 00:02:14.836
don't just say:

00:02:14.836 --> 00:02:15.956
that's not right,

00:02:15.956 --> 00:02:16.756
fix it.

00:02:16.756 --> 00:02:18.316
That's Captain Alex again.

00:02:18.316 --> 00:02:19.166
Be targeted.

00:02:19.196 --> 00:02:21.016
The chart shows monthly totals;

00:02:21.016 --> 00:02:22.086
change it to weekly.

00:02:22.116 --> 00:02:23.076
What's wrong,

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:24.466
what you want instead,

00:02:24.496 --> 00:02:25.686
and how to check it.

00:02:26.261 --> 00:02:27.921
An agent is only as good as

00:02:27.921 --> 00:02:29.191
the job you describe.

00:02:29.321 --> 00:02:31.501
How you explain the job matters more

00:02:31.501 --> 00:02:32.861
than the tool doing it.

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:33.911
Next up:

00:02:34.081 --> 00:02:35.131
context in,

00:02:35.301 --> 00:02:36.591
verification out.
